This is my third slash'em game and since the guy just slew Demogorgon, I 
think that he is going to make it.  AC of -47 also speaks in his favor.


I started playing slash'em some five months ago and played three explore 
mode games at the time.  A flame mage, an ice mage, and a lycanthropic 
rogue.

I lost the flame mage to an irrecoverable game crash.  At that point he 
had already died on the order of 10 times but he had explored the 
dungeon down to the sunless sea and killed Vecna - but only after having 
been killed by him...  So I knew that Vecna is tough.  On the other 
hand, taking him on is attractive, because he will leave a hand that 
gives half physical damage if carried and level drain resistance if 
wielded (only useful for retreating, I suppose).  The other aligned 
quest items, the Eye of the Beholder and Nighthorn, are much less useful.

The ice mage made it to his ascension but had of course also died 
several times before.  But he killed Vecna without having been touched 
by him, even without waking him up, using spells of magic missile and of 
acid stream.  His first encounter with Demogorgon went badly, however. 
Demogorgon seems to have been beefed up in comparison with NH.  At 
least, he took away something like 60 HP or more in one round of melee. 
  One stunning attack and my poor Ice mage could not write Elbereth 
anymore.  Since he had not thought about a good strategy (using potions 
of invulnerability, e.g.) he died once before killing Demogorgon.

I gave up on the lycanthrope, when his max hitpoints were down to 1 due 
to excessively having been killed in polymorphed form...  Probably I 
will have to play quite a number of games before being able to ascend a 
lycanthrope.

Anyway, given the addictive nature of the game, I decided to take a 
three-month break before trying to attempt a real ascension.

In October, I started a doppelganger undead slayer, mostly because 
undead slayers don't exist in NH and I had read that doppelgangers are 
not really difficult. The initial strength of this undead slayer was 9, 
and this was insufficient.  He fought valiantly, but finally died in the 
mines to a scramper and a dragon baby (forgot the color).  I don't 
consider this a stupid death, since he even managed to get out between 
the two using his special ability to liquefy.  From the bones file, my 
current barbarian learned that there were quite a number of scrampers 
running around, so Scroll the undead slayer did not have a chance.

Then I decided that I want something easy, so Spike the doppelganger 
barbarian was born.  He did quite well, but died quite stupidly.  He 
advanced very fast, due to his excessive strength, dexterity and 
constitution.  He was cleaning out the top Sokoban level, when after 
having killed a cockatrice he decided that using its corpse as a weapon 
was an efficient way to proceed.  "Touching the cockatrice corpse was a 
fatal error.  DYWYPI?"  What?  I had never been petrified in a real game 
before...  Well, usually when I am that far down in the dungeon, I have 
gloves already.  This time, because my barbarian advanced so fast, his 
armor was still incomplete.  Normally, after having enchanted armor, I 
always check before picking up a cockatrice, if I am wearing my gloves. 
But I had not yet used any scroll of enchant armor yet...  Since I am 
playing barbarians very rarely (their combat style is monotonous and not 
too interesting), I suddenly found myself without gloves in a situation 
where I usually have gloves already, and not thinking about it this kind 
of death finally happened to me.  I was consternated on the one hand and 
hilarious on the other, telling myself that from now on, I would be much 
more careful about cockatrices...

But for my next try, I would still take a barbarian, not realizing at 
the time that this means essentially no spellcasting.  (I had believed 
that barbarians can at least cast attack spells successfully, but being 
level 21 now, I have so far cast only one spell successfully and 
essentially given up on spells, having found more useful ranged attacks.)

Bjoern the doppelganger barbarian is doing pretty well so far.  He even 
managed to solve one of the Sokoban levels completely that is considered 
only solvable up to one final boulder, not needed to get to the upstairs 
(level 2, version D in Fred Blau's spoiler).  But he had to use his 
doppelganger abilities at one point to get past a row of boulders (does 
liquefying to pass a boulder affect your luck?).  Anyway, afterwards I 
realized that this level is completely solvable for anybody, but that 
the solution involves a move that is not standard in Sokoban...

To get on topic again, I was worrying quite some time about how to deal 
with Vecna.  For this guy is tough, having some 300HP and hitting you 
for some 50HP per turn.  Originally, I was afraid to have to wait until 
I had the ability to repeatedly cast one or two of the attack spells 
that he is vulnerable to and to whack him afterwards, counting on 
potions of invulnerability for myself to survive his hard hitting.
Normally, one would think that firearms are the key, because they do 
heavy damage.  However, from my explore mode games I was under the 
impression that bullets don't do damage to lichs.  But then I gathered 
from an old newsgroup discussion that this must depend on the 
enchantment of the bullets.  It seems that up to an enchantment of one 
they do no damage, starting at two, they do hit.  When I realized this, 
I was quite happy, but I decided to first try it out.  The opportunity 
appeared on the level of the disgruntled adventurers, where a master 
lich turned up, obviously a polymorphed doppelganger (that level can be 
quite deadly, if you don't have level-drain resistance; in particular, 
if you fall in it and can't easily retreat).  First, I switched to my 
trusty +2 assault rifle and sent him a load of +1 bullets.  They did not 
seem to harm him.  Then I switched to the +4 bullets that I had produced 
a little before.  Since I sent 5 or 6 bullets on each salvo, I made him 
return to doppelganger form in the second and then he was history.

I still was nervous about the Vecna level, since I did not want to 
genocide lichs this time, allowing myself to polymorph into a master 
lich or so later.  (One disadvantage of playing a doppelganger 
*barbarian* is that you almost never feel that polymorphing yourself 
would be helpful.  Barbarians are just too strong to try being something 
else.)  The two previous times I had always encountered Vecna alone, no 
other lichs on the level.

Well, I went there without my pets (elementals, cats, dogs), in order to 
be able to carry a ring of conflict to have fewer problems with monsters 
summoned by Vecna.  At first, the arch-lichs turned up.  I was 
surprised, having thought that the level would be infested with lichs 
only.  (Vecna himself does not count as an arch-lich apparently though 
he is stronger than any ordinary arch-lich and behaves mostly like they 
do.)  Fortunately, one of the arch-lichs summoned a cockatrice.  I had 
time to first kill it, since most monsters were hitting each other, and 
to step on its square.  Then I first petrified the umber hulk, lich and 
arch-lich next to me, unwielded the corpse, made a step towards the 
second arch-lich, wielded the corpse and made another statue.  Unwielded 
the corpse, took off the ring of conflict, went through the portal to 
heal the sixty or so lost HP (out of 230).

Back I went.  A question for source divers: If you put on a ring of 
conflict and use your blindfold to see monsters with telepathy, will all 
the monsters you see be affected by conflict?  Or is the range of the 
ring restricted?  The point is that the lichs seemed to have accumulated 
in one place, and it appears that they woke up Vecna.  He teleported 
next to my position, which luckily was in a corridor.  I was so 
surprised that I used Magicbane immediately to engrave Elbereth.  This 
gave me time to switch to my rifle.  Vecna had only 250HP this time and 
my first salvo took him down to about 170HP, as I verified with my 
stethoscope.  Since I fired only five bullets, I assume that they do 
their full damage once the enchantment threshold has been exceeded. 
Apparently, artifacts will hit even when not enchanted, since I had no 
problems killing lichs with the +0 Magicbane.  The rest was easy, after 
two more salvos, Vecna died, leaving his hand.  (It is funny to obtain 
the message that he is "scared", when you apply the stethoscope.)

I had the usual problems on descending further with spellcasters that 
summon monsters.  In the lost tomb, which I had entered surrounded by 
pets, knowing from the last time that there was a ghoul queen waiting 
that would also summon lots of monsters, I was already retreating 
somewhat hastily after my first advances.  On the stair case, surrounded 
by many monsters, including a green dragon and a winged gargoyle, I 
confused myself and read a scroll of tame monster.  I was delighted to 
note that the ghoul queen itself became tame!  I lost her a few levels 
later not knowing where she went.

Since I was neutral, I did not want to kill One-eyed Sam, although I had 
figured out how to do that safely in one of the explore mode games.  I 
just collected all the stuff I wanted, including two magic lamps, went 
to the top left corner of the store, polymorphed into a xorn and left 
the shop without paying.  I felt guilty and one soldier followed me 
through the magic portal.  Since I did not feel like fighting him, I 
teleported away, and my pets who had not been admitted to the portal, 
finished him off.

I should say that Bjoern was very lucky in some respects.  He had gotten 
Frost Brand from a shop (without paying) and he had found a wand of 
wishig in the mines.  When he finally put his hands on the castle wand, 
he did not really need it.  One of the wishes from the four magic lamps 
he owned till now went for a blessed figurine of a planetar.  (Would a 
solar work, too? My strongest pets so far have been planetars.)  Since 
then he has been accompanied by Demon Fighter, his planetar equipped 
with Demonbane.  The planetar has acquired a level of 43 in the 
meantime, which means he will attack most demon lords (excluding of 
course Demogorgon...).

A funny observation: Most of the molds in the Valley of the Dead were 
named Spike, a few Scroll; must have to do with the fact that the game 
has been downloaded not very long ago and there were only two "real" 
games played before this one.

In order to prepare for Demogorgon, I enchanted my blessed unicorn horn 
to +7, took a few blessed potions of full healing and of invulnerability 
from my bag of holding and transferred them to my sack.  Then I 
polymorphed into a ghoul queen, hoping that this way I could avoid 
falling ill by Demo's attack.  But this was not a good idea.  The 
carrying capacity of a ghoul queen is so meager that I would have had to 
leave most stuff behind, including my rifle and bullets.  So I decided 
that I would have to heal my illness with full healing and (blessed) 
eucalyptus leaves and returned to doppelganger form.

After having left the staircase on Demo's level I realized that I had 
forgotten to put some item on it and write "Elbereth".  But I did not 
want to return, thinking that I was going to read a cursed scroll of 
teleportation anyway and coming down the stairs when Demogorgon was 
sitting on them, he would have to retreat...  Well, there were still 
some surprises for me.  I knew that he would see me as soon as I turned 
around the corner.  But since the last time he had not teleported to me 
immediately but first summoned a lot of monsters, I did not quaff my 
potion of invulnerability immediately, because I did not want to waste 
any one of the precious turns I would be invulnerable.  He teleported 
directly to me, hit me, made me ill and took away 60 or 70 HP.  Now the 
dilemma was which potion to drink first.  I had only two outside my bag, 
because he might have summoned pyrolisks or comparable monsters, boiling 
my potions away.  If I drank full healing, he would hit me again, taking 
more HP and making me ill the second time.  If I drank invulnerability, 
I would get a second illness hit.  Fortunately, he had not stunned me, 
so I ended up cowardly engraving Elbereth again.  Actually, I think the 
fact that Demogorgon and Pestilence will stun you, if their second 
illness attack in one turn gets through, makes things worse, not better, 
because ill and stunned, you have much fewer defense options.  Full 
healing will not take away the stunning (I believe) and unicorn horns 
may take away only the stunning or only the illness, which gives another 
turn to Demo for attack.  But now I had bought time.  I quaffed both 
potions and shot Demogorgon; he teleported away, I teleported one level 
up (to the VotD).  Then I took out another potion of full healing went 
down, engraved Elbereth again, healed myself, and shot another salvo at 
Demo, who had only 150HP left.  I was now sure to get him.  Well, he 
escaped upstairs, and I realized only then that it was not me that had 
displaced him but vice versa (is this always so? if so, it is more or 
less mandatory to put down an item and engrave Elbereth on the stairs).
I followed him, prepared to teleport to the upstairs in the VotD (which 
is not a no-teleport level in slash'em). He was next to me and I decided 
that one additional salvo must be sufficient for him.  So it was indeed. 
  He died after the second of five bullets...

Now I have to watch out for those diamond and sapphire golems mostly, 
all other single monsters should not be too dangerous.  Having played an 
explore mode game before was immensely helpful.  Otherwise, I would not 
have had a precise idea what to expect in Gehennom.
